# ''Service owners'' — [[Tjenesteeiere]] [service owners] is there, but no service has a `tjenesteeier` [service owner] yet. Where do we get the list of services → owners? A spreadsheet is fine. Until then the owner column in the ToDo lists and the owner grouping under //Tjenester// [services] are empty.
# ''Data cleanup'' — [[Anomalier]] [anomalies] lists ten classes of problem with the affected tiddlers. Two are now fixable from the page itself. These changes rewrite your data, so we want approval per class. Nothing runs until someone presses a button; a merge retags and never deletes; on a server each one is a file write per tiddler, with no automatic undo.
#* ''Casing drift — which spelling is correct, per family?'' Ten tags in five families. Four look like typing slips and we would merge onto the majority: `Juni`/`juni` (212 vs 4 tiddlers), `April`/`april` (189 vs 4), `Resultat`/`resultat` (435 vs 2), `MyHealth@EU`/`Myhealth@EU` (3 vs 1).
#* The fifth is a real decision: `Ekstern Tjeneste` [external service] is on 95 tiddlers and `Ekstern tjeneste` on 22, so the majority says capital T — but the rest of the taxonomy is lower case, including the `Styring …` tags and the //Tjeneste// form, which only ever offers `Ekstern tjeneste`. Keeping the capital retags 22 tiddlers; keeping the lower case retags 95 and renames the tiddler `Ekstern Tjeneste`. Which is right?
#* ''The other classes — which may we fix?'' Self-tagging and tag cycles (87 tiddlers); bullet lists glued to the paragraph above, which TiddlyWiki renders as literal `*` text (31); leftover edit drafts, already excluded from every count but still in the wiki (19, of which 18 can be deleted with one click on the page; the nineteenth holds text that exists nowhere else, so we leave it for you); tiddlers tagged both `Målsetting` [objective] and `Resultat` [key result], so their type is ambiguous (3); monthly reviews carrying a service-type tag, which makes them look like services (2); `Tiltak` [initiative] and `Satsning for fart` tagged straight onto `Norsk Helsenett SF` (2).
# ''Archiving'' — [[Arkiv]] [archive] hides prior years behind an `Arkiv` tag, reversibly, without deleting anything. Is that enough, or should old years leave the live wiki entirely (a separate read-only archive wiki)?
# ''Business-review rules'' — the ToDo list counts a review as done only if its text differs from the template and from last month's review. Is an exact comparison right, or should a review that is a near-copy be flagged too? And should `Forretningsmessig endring` [business change, i.e. severity] be required on a new review? 76% of existing reviews lack it, so we made it optional.
# ''Which services should report?'' — the ToDo lists default to the services that have filed at least one review this year: 43 of the 60 services in the wiki have for 2026. The other 17 only appear under the //Alle tjenester// [all services] toggle, so a service that has never reported is never chased. Is that the right rule, or should the list of services expected to report be maintained explicitly?
# ''Permalink base'' — is `https://tiddlywiki.plattform.nhn.no/#…` correct?
# ''Authorisation'' — when run as a server, the wiki is currently open to all readers and writers. Should it be restricted to NHN staff? If so, how? Two options are:
#* ''Auth proxy in front of the server'' - a reverse proxy on the hosting platform authenticates each user against AD (OIDC / SAML / ADFS) and passes the verified username to TiddlyWiki in a request header. The sever supports the `readers` and `writers` lists of who may read and who may save
#* ''Two deployments'' — the server for editors, and the static single-file build for everyone else, regenerated on a schedule.
# ''Deliveries with no service link'' — [[Leveranserapport]] [delivery report] groups deliveries under the service they are tagged with, and lists the ones it cannot place. For mai–august 2026 that is 12 of 75, the same number your own script reported. Looking at them, they are two different situations:
#* ''The service is one hop away'' (8 of the 12) — the delivery is tagged a `Målsetting` [objective] such as `Sektorkontakt` or `Forutsigbarhet av tjenester og funksjonalitet for leverandørene`, and that objective is itself tagged the service (`Støtte og hjelp til leverandører`, `Helsenettet.no og Medlemstjenesten`). Nothing is wrong with the tagging — the report simply does not follow the hop. Should it, or should a delivery always carry its service tag directly? If the former, we change the report and your data stays as it is.
#* ''Work that belongs to a national initiative, not a service'' (4) — the Parek and DHG deliveries are tagged `Pasientens rekvisisjoner` (`Satsning for fart` [drive for speed]) and `Tiltaket digitalt helsekort for gravide` (`Tiltak` [initiative]). Those sit on `Norsk Helsenett SF`, above the divisions, so no service owns them. Should the board report carry a section for initiatives alongside the services, or should these deliveries also be tagged the service that does the work?
#* Separately: three of these deliveries carry `Støtte og hjelp til leverandør`, singular, where the service is `Støtte og hjelp til leverandører`, plural. That tag matches nothing at all and is worth correcting whatever you decide above.
# ''Reporting period'' — the report covers a range of months within one calendar year, which is what your own script did. Do any reporting periods cross a year boundary? And can we have the output of your script for one period, so we can compare it to ours line by line rather than only by count?
# ''Backfill `period`'' — new tiddlers created through the forms are stamped with a `period` field (`2026-03` monthly, `2026` yearly). No existing content carries one — 2209 periodic tiddlers, of which 867 are reviews. May we run a one-off pass that stamps them from their year and month tags? It makes every period filter cheaper and less dependent on tag spelling.
# ''Deployment'' — where does the wiki run once this is finished, and who updates it? Today there is a Node.js server for editing and a single-file build for reading. We can document either, but we need to know which one NHN will operate.
